+ # Contract Variant Templates
+ Five base patterns for the most common ecommerce creator deals. Pick the variant that matches the collaboration type, then customize.
+ ## Variant A — Product-for-Post (Gifted)
+ **Use when**: No cash changes hands; Brand sends product worth under ~$200 in exchange for organic content.
+ **Must-have clauses**:
+ **Must-have clauses**:
+ **Must-have clauses**:
+ **Must-have clauses**:
+ - Deliverables (1 post is typical)
+ - Required disclosure (#gifted or #ad in first 3 lines)
+ - Organic usage rights, perpetual on Brand-owned channels with Creator credit
+ - 30-day non-deletion clause (post must remain live)
+ - No exclusivity (or category-only, 14 days)
+ - No kill fee
+ **Skip**: paid rights, performance bonuses, complex revision rounds.
+ ## Variant B — Flat-Fee UGC
+ **Use when**: Brand pays a flat fee for one or more pieces of content. May or may not include posting on Creator's own channel.
+ - Specific deliverables with format/length spec
+ - 2 revision rounds with 48-hour SLA each
+ - Full transfer of usage rights for Brand-owned channels (organic + paid for N months)
+ - Kill fee schedule (50% pre-shoot, 100% post-shoot)
+ - Payment schedule (50% on signing, 50% on approval)
+ ## Variant C — Affiliate Partnership
+ **Use when**: Compensation is variable, tied to tracked sales via discount code or affiliate link.
+ - Commission rate, attribution window (typically 30 days), payment cadence
+ - Net Sales definition (gross less returns/refunds/tax/shipping)
+ - Posting cadence (e.g., minimum 1 post per month for 3 months)
+ - Code/link issuance and tracking platform (e.g., Refersion, Impact)
+ - Termination: either side may end with 30 days' notice
+ **Often skipped**: kill fee (no upfront fee to kill), exclusivity.
+ ## Variant D — Whitelisted Paid Ads
+ **Use when**: Brand will run the content as paid ads via Creator's handle (Spark Ads, Branded Content tool, etc.).
+ - Whitelisting access mechanism and duration (Spark Ads code maintained for full window)
+ - Paid usage rights for [N] months, optional renewal at agreed rate
+ - Specific platforms (TikTok, Instagram, both)
+ - Higher fee than equivalent organic-only deal (paid rights are valuable)
+ - Asset handover: raw footage, vertical and square crops
+ ## Variant E — Hybrid (Multi-Component)
+ **Use when**: Combination of product gift + flat fee + paid usage + affiliate commission.
+ **Must-have clauses**: All of Variants B and D, plus:
+ - Affiliate commission terms layered on top (modest %, since flat fee already paid)
+ - Performance bonus tied to attributable sales or specific view threshold
+ - Clear stack of compensation: product + fee + commission + bonus
+ ---
+ ## Variant Selection Quick Test
+ | Brand pays cash? | Brand wants paid ads? | Variable comp? | → Variant |
+ |------------------|----------------------|----------------|-----------|
+ | No | No | No | A — Gifted |
+ | Yes | No | No | B — Flat-Fee UGC |
+ | Optional | No | Yes | C — Affiliate |
+ | Yes | Yes | No | D — Whitelisted |
+ | Yes | Yes | Yes | E — Hybrid |

### references/jurisdiction-flags.md  [docs]
+ # Jurisdiction Flags
+ What changes by region. The contract draft can flag these but cannot localize automatically — surface the items below in a "Localize before signing" callout.
+ ## United States
+ - **FTC Endorsement Guides (16 CFR Part 255)**: clear and conspicuous disclosure required. #ad in the first 3 caption lines is the safer minimum; #sponsored or #partner is acceptable; "thanks for the gift" alone is not.
+ - **California AB 5 / Borello test**: Creator may be classified as employed not contractor in some setups. Single-deal collaborations are typically safe; ongoing exclusive arrangements may not be.
+ - **Tax**: 1099-NEC if payments exceed $600/year. W-9 collection at signing.
+ ## United Kingdom
+ - **ASA / CAP**: paid-for content must be disclosed via Instagram's "Paid partnership" tool or #ad at start of caption. ASA is aggressive on enforcement.
+ - **HMRC**: VAT may apply if Creator is VAT-registered.
+ - **IR35**: relevant for ongoing engagements; less so for single-deal collaborations.
+ ## European Union
+ - **AGCM (Italy), DGCCRF (France), Bundeskartellamt (Germany)**: vary in detail but converge on requirement for clear paid disclosure.
+ - **VAT**: B2B reverse charge if Creator is VAT-registered in another EU state.
+ - **GDPR**: data shared in the contract (real names, addresses) is personal data; minimize sharing.
+ ## Brazil
+ - **CONAR**: self-regulatory; clear disclosure required (#publi or #publicidade).
+ - **Tax**: withholding may apply for foreign brands paying Brazilian creators.
+ ## Southeast Asia (TikTok-heavy)
+ - **Singapore**: ASAS guidelines, similar to ASA.
+ - **Indonesia (Shopee, TikTok Shop)**: BPOM rules for cosmetics, supplements; halal certification claims must be backed.
+ - **Philippines / Vietnam**: nascent regulation; brand reputational risk drives stricter self-policing.
+ ## Australia
+ - **AANA / ACCC**: paid disclosure required; "ad" or "advertisement" is preferred over emoji-only disclosures.
+ - **Tax**: ABN required for the Creator, withholding if no ABN.
+ ---
+ ## Disclosure Phrasing — Acceptable vs Risky
+ | Phrasing | Verdict |
+ |----------|---------|
+ | "#ad" in first 3 lines + spoken disclosure | Strong everywhere |
+ | "Paid partnership with @brand" via platform tool | Strong (Instagram, TikTok) |
+ | "#sponsored" | Acceptable in most regions |
+ | "#thanks @brand for the gift" only | Risky — implies no compensation expectation |
+ | Buried disclosure (line 8 of caption) | Risky — fails the "clear and conspicuous" test |
+ | Emoji-only (e.g., 💸) | Risky / not accepted in UK, AU, EU |
+ ## Withholding Tax Reminder
+ When a US brand pays a non-US creator, request W-8BEN to claim treaty benefits and reduce withholding (typically 30% default, often 0–10% under treaty).

### references/output-template.md  [docs]
+ # Output Template — Creator Collaboration Agreement
+ Use this section structure for every contract draft. Each numbered heading should be followed by a one-line plain-English summary, then the contract language.
+ ---
+ ---
+ ## 1. Parties and Scope
+ *Plain-English: Identifies who is signing and what work is covered.*
+ This Creator Collaboration Agreement ("Agreement") is entered into on [DATE] between [BRAND NAME], a [STATE] [ENTITY TYPE] ("Brand"), and [CREATOR NAME / LEGAL ENTITY] ("Creator"). The Agreement covers the deliverables described in Section 2 and the rights, obligations, and compensation set forth herein.
+ ## 2. Deliverables and Timeline
+ *Plain-English: What the creator must produce and by when.*
+ | Deliverable | Platform | Format | Quantity | Posting Window |
+ |-------------|----------|--------|----------|----------------|
+ | [e.g., Product demo] | TikTok | Video, 30–60s | 1 | Within 14 days of product receipt |
+ Creator shall submit a draft to Brand at least [X] business days before each scheduled posting date.
+ ## 3. Content Specifications and Approvals
+ *Plain-English: Quality requirements and the review process.*
+ - Creator shall include the following hashtags / disclosures: [list]
+ - Creator shall mention the following talking points: [list]
+ - Approval rounds: [N] rounds, [SLA] business days for Brand review and Creator revision per round
+ - Final asset format and resolution: [spec]
+ ## 4. Usage Rights
+ *Plain-English: Where, how, and how long Brand can use the content.*
+ Creator grants Brand:
+ - (a) **Organic rights**: [perpetual / N months] on Brand-owned channels
+ - (b) **Paid rights**: [N months] for paid social media, including [whitelisting tool, e.g., Spark Ads / Branded Content]
+ - (c) **Repurposing rights**: [list channels — website, email, retail signage]
+ ## 5. Exclusivity
+ *Plain-English: Categories or competitors the creator can't work with during a window.*
+ For [N] days from first publication, Creator shall not promote: [category definition] OR [named competitor list].
+ ## 6. Compensation and Payment
+ *Plain-English: How and when the creator gets paid.*
+ - Flat fee: $[X], paid [50% on signing / 100% on approval / etc.]
+ - Product value: $[X], shipped within [N] days
+ - Performance bonus (if applicable): [tier table tied to UTM/discount-code sales or view threshold]
+ - Affiliate commission (if applicable): [%] of Net Sales, [N]-day cookie, paid monthly net [N]
+ - Kill fee: [% pre-shoot] / [% post-shoot]
+ ## 7. Representations and Warranties
+ *Plain-English: Promises both sides make about the content.*
+ Creator represents that the Content will be original, will not infringe third-party rights (including music), and will comply with applicable disclosure rules (FTC 16 CFR Part 255 in the US, ASA/CAP in the UK, equivalent rules elsewhere). Creator shall include #ad in the first three lines of caption and verbal disclosure where format permits.
+ ## 8. Termination and Kill Fees
+ *Plain-English: How either side ends the deal early.*
+ Either party may terminate for material breach with [N] days' written notice and opportunity to cure. If Brand terminates without cause after Creator has begun production but before publication, Brand shall pay [%] of the flat fee. If terminated after publication, full fee applies.
+ ## 9. General Provisions
+ *Plain-English: Standard legal boilerplate.*
+ Governing law: [STATE/COUNTRY]. Disputes: [arbitration / courts]. Amendments must be in writing. This Agreement is the entire agreement between the parties.
+ ## Negotiation Cheat Sheet
+ Append a 3–4 item cheat sheet flagging clauses likely to be pushed back on, each with a suggested fallback position.
+ ## Localize Before Signing
+ Append a short callout listing items that vary by jurisdiction and must be confirmed locally.

### references/usage-rights-glossary.md  [docs]
+ # Usage Rights Glossary
+ Plain-English definitions for the rights vocabulary that shows up in every creator contract. Use these to write the Plain-English summary lines in the contract draft.
+ ## Organic Rights
+ The Brand can post the Creator's content on Brand-owned channels (Instagram, TikTok, YouTube, website, email) as a regular non-promoted post. Standard for almost every deal. Almost always granted in perpetuity for Brand-owned channels with Creator credit.
+ ## Paid Rights
+ The Brand can run the content as a paid ad. Where the ad runs and for how long is the negotiation. Three sub-types:
+ - **Brand-handle paid**: ad runs from Brand's own ad account / handle
+ - **Whitelisted paid**: ad runs from Creator's handle (looks like organic from the audience's view) via Spark Ads (TikTok), Branded Content tool (Instagram), or YouTube partnership ads
+ - **Programmatic / off-platform paid**: ad runs on Google, Meta Audience Network, programmatic display
+ Rule of thumb: paid rights cost 30–100% on top of the equivalent organic-only fee, depending on duration.
+ ## Whitelisting
+ Specific to paid rights. The Creator gives the Brand limited access to their account so the Brand can run paid ads from the Creator's handle. Requires:
+ - Spark Ads access code (TikTok) or partnership invite (Instagram Branded Content)
+ - Duration of access (must match paid-rights window)
+ - Reporting access for the Brand to monitor performance
+ Whitelisting amplifies paid-rights value because audience trust is higher when the ad appears to come from a real creator.
+ ## Repurposing Rights
+ Use of the content outside its original platform: website hero, email creative, retail signage, packaging, podcast clip, etc. Brand should always ask for repurposing rights for owned channels; pricier creators may carve out specific use cases.
+ ## Perpetual Rights
+ No end date. The Brand can use the content forever. Standard for organic on Brand-owned channels; expensive (and resisted) for paid use. If the Brand wants perpetual paid, it should pay an uplift (often 50–100% of the flat fee).
+ ## Exclusivity vs Usage Rights
+ Easy to confuse:
+ - **Usage rights** = what the Brand can do with this specific content
+ - **Exclusivity** = what the Creator cannot do with their channel during a window (post for competitors)
+ A deal can be exclusive without granting paid rights, or vice versa.
+ ## Term-of-Use Vocabulary Cheat Sheet
+ | Phrase | What it means |
+ |--------|---------------|
+ | "Worldwide, perpetual, royalty-free" | Brand can use the content anywhere, forever, with no further payment |
+ | "Non-exclusive" | Brand has rights, but Creator can also use the content |
+ | "Sublicensable" | Brand can grant the rights to a third party (e.g., agency, retail partner) |
+ | "First-look window" | Creator must offer Brand the next campaign before competitors for [N] days |
+ | "Right of first refusal" | Brand can match a competing offer to retain the Creator |
+ | "Buyout" | One-time payment for full rights, usually perpetual and unlimited |